  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the high wall shear stress of first-order human retinal arterioles (~1.5 Pa / 15 dyn/cm²) in a perfused microfluidic chip.",
    "rationale": "First-order human retinal arterioles are a vascular bed known for unusually high wall shear stress, reported around 10-20 dyn/cm² (1-2 Pa), roughly an order of magnitude above typical microvascular endothelium (0.1-1 Pa). I target 1.5 Pa (15 dyn/cm²) as a representative physiological value. A 400 µm wide x 100 µm high x 20 mm long PDMS channel at 60 µL/min delivers 1.5 Pa wall shear (verified via flow_rate_for_shear_stress), with Re = 4 (firmly laminar), residence time 0.8 s, and pressure drop ~600 Pa (well within syringe-pump range). The registry maps retinal microvascular endothelial cells to HUVEC physiology (shear 0.1-1 Pa, seed 1e4-2e4 cells/cm²); the retinal arteriole target intentionally exceeds the generic HUVEC range to reflect the high-shear retinal bed. Channel floor area = 0.08 cm², so seeding ~1200-1600 cells (15-20k/cm²) gives a confluent monolayer before perfusion. Culture medium viscosity assumed water-like (1e-3 Pa·s).",
    "chip": {
      "width_um": 400.0,
      "height_um": 100.0,
      "length_mm": 20.0,
      "channel_count": 1,
      "material": "PDMS"
    },
